{"subjectId":"python","id":"numbers","title":"Numbers: int, float, Decimal & Fraction","group":"Strings & Numbers","order":13,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Python has unlimited-size integers, IEEE-754 binary floats, exact <code>Decimal</code> and <code>Fraction</code> types and a rich <code>math</code> module. Picking the right one avoids rounding bugs and overflow.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Precision</th><th>Speed</th><th>Use for</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>int</code></td><td>Exact, <strong>unlimited</strong> size</td><td>Fast</td><td>Counting, indices, IDs, big-number math</td></tr><tr><td><code>float</code></td><td>~15–17 significant digits (64-bit binary)</td><td>Fastest (hardware)</td><td>Science, measurements, ML — where tiny errors are fine</td></tr><tr><td><code>decimal.Decimal</code></td><td>Exact decimal, configurable precision</td><td>Slower</td><td><strong>Money</strong>, financial rounding rules</td></tr><tr><td><code>fractions.Fraction</code></td><td>Exact rational <code>p/q</code></td><td>Slowest</td><td>Exact ratios, probability, teaching</td></tr><tr><td><code>complex</code></td><td>Pair of floats</td><td>Fast</td><td>Signal processing, maths (<code>2 + 3j</code>)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-number\"></i>Integers: unlimited &amp; many literals</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>print(2 ** 100)                               # 1267650600228229401496703205376\nprint(0b1010, 0o17, 0xFF, 1_000_000)          # 10 15 255 1000000\nprint(int(\"1010\", 2), int(\"z\", 36), int(\" 42 \"))   # 10 35 42\nn = 37\nprint(n.bit_length(), n.bit_count(), bin(n))  # 6 3 0b100101\nprint(7 // 2, -7 // 2, -7 % 3, int(-3.9))     # 3 -4 2 -3\nprint(divmod(17, 5), divmod(-17, 5))          # (3, 2) (-4, 3)</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p><code>//</code> and <code>%</code> round toward <strong>negative infinity</strong>, so <code>-7 // 2 == -4</code> and <code>-7 % 3 == 2</code> (the result has the sign of the divisor). C and Java truncate toward zero. <code>int()</code> truncates toward zero.</p></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>print(pow(2, 10), pow(2, 10, 1000))    # 1024 24     ← fast modular exponentiation\nprint(pow(3, -1, 7))                   # 5           ← modular inverse: 3 * 5 % 7 == 1\nprint(True + True, isinstance(True, int))   # 2 True\nprint((255).to_bytes(2, \"big\"), int.from_bytes(b\"\\x01\\x00\", \"big\"))   # b'\\x00\\xff' 256</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>Converting a huge <code>int</code> to/from <code>str</code> is limited to 4300 digits by default (a DoS protection since 3.11): <code>str(10**5000)</code> raises <code>ValueError</code>. Raise it with <code>sys.set_int_max_str_digits(n)</code> if you really need to.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-alert-triangle\"></i>Floats are binary approximations</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>print(0.1 + 0.2)                        # 0.30000000000000004\nprint(0.1 + 0.2 == 0.3)                 # False\nimport math\nprint(math.isclose(0.1 + 0.2, 0.3))     # True\nprint((0.1).as_integer_ratio())         # (3602879701896397, 36028797018963968)\nprint(f\"{0.1:.20f}\")                    # 0.10000000000000000555\ntotal = 0.0\nfor _ in range(10):\n    total += 0.1                        # error accumulates step by step\nprint(total, math.fsum([0.1] * 10))     # 0.9999999999999999 1.0\nprint(sum([0.1] * 10))                  # 1.0   ← sum() of floats is compensated since 3.12\nprint(2 ** 53 + 1.0 == 2 ** 53)         # True   ← integers above 2**53 lose precision</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--def\"><p><strong>IEEE 754 double</strong> — 64 bits: 1 sign bit, 11 exponent bits, 52 fraction bits. Only numbers of the form <em>m × 2ᵉ</em> are exact, so decimal fractions like 0.1 are stored as the nearest binary value.</p></div>\n<div class=\"grid\"><div class=\"callout callout--good\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Do</div><p>Compare floats with <code>math.isclose(a, b, rel_tol=1e-9, abs_tol=…)</code>; sum many floats with <code>math.fsum</code>; keep money in integer cents or <code>Decimal</code>.</p></div><div class=\"callout callout--bad\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Avoid</div><p><code>if total == 0.3:</code> or storing prices as floats — errors accumulate and comparisons fail.</p></div></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-infinity\"></i>inf, nan &amp; float limits</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import math, sys\ninf, nan = float(\"inf\"), float(\"nan\")\nprint(inf &gt; 10**308, -inf &lt; 0, inf - inf)     # True True nan\nprint(nan == nan, math.isnan(nan))            # False True\nprint(1e308 * 10, 2.0 ** -1075)               # inf 0.0\nprint(sys.float_info.max, sys.float_info.epsilon)   # 1.7976931348623157e+308 2.220446049250313e-16\nprint(sorted([3, nan, 1]))                    # [3, nan, 1]   ← nan breaks sorting</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p><code>nan</code> is not equal to anything, including itself, so <code>x in list</code>, <code>sort</code>, <code>max</code> and dict lookups behave strangely with it. Filter with <code>math.isnan</code> first. Also <code>1 / 0</code> raises <code>ZeroDivisionError</code> — Python does not return <code>inf</code> for division.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-up-down\"></i>Rounding</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import math\nprint(round(2.5), round(3.5), round(-2.5))       # 2 4 -2   ← banker's rounding\nprint(round(2.675, 2))                           # 2.67     ← 2.675 is really 2.67499999…\nprint(round(1234.5678, -2))                      # 1200.0\nprint(math.floor(-2.5), math.ceil(-2.5), math.trunc(-2.5))   # -3 -2 -2\nprint(int(2.99), math.floor(2.99))               # 2 2</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Function</th><th>Rounds</th><th><code>2.5</code></th><th><code>-2.5</code></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>round(x)</code></td><td>To nearest, ties to <strong>even</strong></td><td><code>2</code></td><td><code>-2</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>math.floor(x)</code></td><td>Down (toward −∞)</td><td><code>2</code></td><td><code>-3</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>math.ceil(x)</code></td><td>Up (toward +∞)</td><td><code>3</code></td><td><code>-2</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>math.trunc(x)</code> / <code>int(x)</code></td><td>Toward zero</td><td><code>2</code></td><td><code>-2</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>Decimal.quantize(…, ROUND_HALF_UP)</code></td><td>Ties away from zero (school rounding)</td><td><code>3</code></td><td><code>-3</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-coin-rupee\"></i>Decimal: exact decimal arithmetic</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>from decimal import Decimal, ROUND_HALF_UP, getcontext, localcontext\nprint(Decimal(\"0.1\") + Decimal(\"0.2\"))               # 0.3\nprint(Decimal(0.1))                                  # 0.1000000000000000055511151231257827021181583404541015625\nprice = Decimal(\"19.99\")\ntotal = price * 3\nprint(total, total * Decimal(\"1.18\"))                # 59.97 70.7646\ntax = (total * Decimal(\"0.18\")).quantize(Decimal(\"0.01\"), rounding=ROUND_HALF_UP)\nprint(tax)                                           # 10.79\nprint(Decimal(\"2.5\").quantize(Decimal(\"1\"), rounding=ROUND_HALF_UP))   # 3\nprint(getcontext().prec)                             # 28\nwith localcontext() as ctx:\n    ctx.prec = 5\n    print(Decimal(1) / Decimal(7))                   # 0.14286</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>Build <code>Decimal</code> from <strong>strings</strong> (<code>Decimal('0.1')</code>), never from floats — <code>Decimal(0.1)</code> faithfully copies the float's binary error. Also don't mix <code>Decimal</code> and <code>float</code> in arithmetic: it raises <code>TypeError</code>.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-divide\"></i>Fraction &amp; complex</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>from fractions import Fraction\nf = Fraction(1, 3) + Fraction(1, 6)\nprint(f, f.numerator, f.denominator)            # 1/2 1 2\nprint(Fraction(\"0.75\"), Fraction(0.1).limit_denominator(100))   # 3/4 1/10\nprint(float(Fraction(22, 7)))                   # 3.142857142857143\n\nz = 3 + 4j\nprint(z.real, z.imag, abs(z), z.conjugate())    # 3.0 4.0 5.0 (3-4j)\nimport cmath\nprint(cmath.sqrt(-1))                           # 1j</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-math-function\"></i>The <code>math</code> module</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Function</th><th>Does</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>sqrt</code>, <code>isqrt</code></td><td>Float root / exact integer floor root</td><td><code>math.isqrt(17)</code> → <code>4</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>gcd</code>, <code>lcm</code></td><td>Greatest common divisor / least common multiple (any number of args)</td><td><code>math.lcm(4, 6)</code> → <code>12</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>comb</code>, <code>perm</code>, <code>factorial</code></td><td>nCr, nPr, n!</td><td><code>math.comb(5, 2)</code> → <code>10</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>prod</code>, <code>fsum</code></td><td>Product / precise float sum</td><td><code>math.prod([2, 3, 4])</code> → <code>24</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>log</code>, <code>log2</code>, <code>log10</code>, <code>exp</code></td><td>Logarithms (<code>log(x, base)</code>)</td><td><code>math.log2(1024)</code> → <code>10.0</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>hypot</code>, <code>dist</code></td><td>Euclidean length / distance between points</td><td><code>math.dist((0, 0), (3, 4))</code> → <code>5.0</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>floor</code>, <code>ceil</code>, <code>trunc</code></td><td>Rounding to int</td><td><code>math.ceil(2.1)</code> → <code>3</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>pi</code>, <code>e</code>, <code>tau</code>, <code>inf</code>, <code>nan</code></td><td>Constants</td><td><code>math.tau == 2 * math.pi</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>isclose</code>, <code>isfinite</code>, <code>isnan</code></td><td>Float checks</td><td><code>math.isclose(0.1 + 0.2, 0.3)</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import math\nprint(math.isqrt(10**20), math.sqrt(2))        # 10000000000 1.4142135623730951\nprint(math.gcd(12, 18, 30), math.lcm(4, 6))    # 6 12\nprint(math.comb(52, 5), math.perm(5, 2), math.factorial(10))   # 2598960 20 3628800\nprint(math.prod(range(1, 6)), math.log2(1024), math.log(8, 2))  # 120 10.0 3.0\nprint(math.hypot(3, 4), math.dist((1, 1), (4, 5)))   # 5.0 5.0\nprint(math.degrees(math.pi), round(math.sin(math.pi / 6), 3))   # 180.0 0.5</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>Use <code>math.isqrt(n)</code> — not <code>int(math.sqrt(n))</code> — for perfect-square checks on big integers: the float version is wrong once <code>n</code> exceeds 2⁵³.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-message-question\"></i>Interview questions</h3>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Why is 0.1 + 0.2 != 0.3?</summary><div><p>Floats are binary (IEEE 754). 0.1, 0.2 and 0.3 have no exact binary representation, so each is stored as the nearest double; the rounding errors don't cancel. Compare with <code>math.isclose</code>, or use <code>Decimal</code> / integer cents when exactness matters.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Can Python integers overflow?</summary><div><p>No. <code>int</code> is arbitrary precision — it grows to use as much memory as needed (slower as it grows). Floats can overflow to <code>inf</code>, and <code>numpy</code> fixed-width integers <em>do</em> wrap around.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What does round(2.5) return and why?</summary><div><p><code>2</code>. Python 3 uses round-half-to-even (banker's rounding), which avoids a systematic upward bias when rounding many values. Use <code>Decimal.quantize(..., rounding=ROUND_HALF_UP)</code> for school-style rounding.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>How would you check whether a large number is a perfect square?</summary><div><figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import math\ndef is_square(n):\n    return n &gt;= 0 and math.isqrt(n) ** 2 == n\nprint(is_square(10**30), is_square(10**30 + 1))   # True False</code></pre></figure></div></details></section>"}